1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,005
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,455 --> 00:00:07,611
This is High School Physics, Unit 1,
Measurement and Motion Basics, Part 1. In

3
00:00:07,611 --> 00:00:11,842
this video, we'll cover four topics, each
with a worked example.

4
00:00:12,292 --> 00:00:15,001
Why Physics? Models, Measurement and SI
Units.

5
00:00:15,451 --> 00:00:19,483
Scientific Notation, Significant Figures
and Unit Conversion.

6
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:22,984
Scalars versus Vectors: Distance and
Displacement.

7
00:00:23,434 --> 00:00:24,735
Speed and Velocity.

8
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:26,273
Let's get started.

9
00:00:27,916 --> 00:00:32,725
Why does a ball fall? How fast does light
travel? How much energy does a phone

10
00:00:32,725 --> 00:00:33,527
battery hold?

11
00:00:33,977 --> 00:00:38,296
Physics is the science that answers
questions like these, using careful

12
00:00:38,296 --> 00:00:40,121
measurement and simple models.

13
00:00:41,271 --> 00:00:45,623
A model is a simplified picture of reality
that we can calculate with.

14
00:00:46,073 --> 00:00:50,553
For a thrown ball, we might ignore the
air, and treat the ball as a single point.

15
00:00:51,003 --> 00:00:54,608
The model isn't perfect, but it predicts
the ball's path very well.

16
00:00:55,758 --> 00:00:58,723
Every measurement has two parts: a number,
and a unit.

17
00:00:59,173 --> 00:01:01,691
Saying a table is two long tells us
nothing.

18
00:01:02,141 --> 00:01:06,130
Two meters, two feet, and two centimeters
are very different tables.

19
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,248
Scientists everywhere use the same system
of units, called S I.

20
00:01:11,698 --> 00:01:16,161
The three you'll use most are the meter
for length, the kilogram for mass, and the

21
00:01:16,161 --> 00:01:17,031
second for time.

22
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:22,160
Other units are built from these. For
example, speed is measured in meters per

23
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:22,580
second.

24
00:01:23,730 --> 00:01:27,570
For very large or very small amounts, S I
uses prefixes.

25
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:32,095
Kilo means one thousand, so a kilometer is
one thousand meters.

26
00:01:32,545 --> 00:01:35,809
Centi means one hundredth, and milli means
one thousandth.

27
00:01:36,959 --> 00:01:39,860
Let's convert two point five kilometers to
meters.

28
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:45,426
One kilometer is one thousand meters, so
we multiply two point five by one

29
00:01:45,426 --> 00:01:46,049
thousand.

30
00:01:46,499 --> 00:01:48,781
That gives two thousand five hundred
meters.

31
00:01:49,931 --> 00:01:51,297
Now try one yourself.

32
00:01:51,747 --> 00:01:56,189
A student sits three hundred fifty
centimeters from the board. How far is

33
00:01:56,189 --> 00:01:57,101
that in meters?

34
00:01:57,551 --> 00:01:58,746
Pause the video now.

35
00:01:59,196 --> 00:02:04,876
One meter is one hundred centimeters, so
divide by one hundred. The answer is three

36
00:02:04,876 --> 00:02:06,108
point five meters.

37
00:02:06,408 --> 00:02:09,565
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

38
00:02:10,715 --> 00:02:15,835
A table is one hundred fifty centimeters
long and seventy five centimeters wide.

39
00:02:16,285 --> 00:02:18,781
Find its area in SI units, square meters.

40
00:02:19,931 --> 00:02:24,411
The SI unit of length is the meter, so
convert each length to meters first.

41
00:02:24,861 --> 00:02:26,504
Then multiply length by width.

42
00:02:27,654 --> 00:02:32,842
Length: one hundred fifty centimeters
divided by one hundred is one point five

43
00:02:32,842 --> 00:02:33,307
meters.

44
00:02:33,757 --> 00:02:37,917
Width: seventy five centimeters is zero
point seven five meters.

45
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:43,709
Area: one point five times zero point
seven five, which is one point one two

46
00:02:43,709 --> 00:02:45,045
five square meters.

47
00:02:45,495 --> 00:02:50,500
Check another way: in centimeters, the
area is eleven thousand two hundred fifty

48
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:55,818
square centimeters. One square meter holds
ten thousand square centimeters, so that's

49
00:02:55,818 --> 00:02:58,508
one point one two five square meters
again.

50
00:02:59,658 --> 00:03:03,007
The table's area is about one point one
square meters.

51
00:03:03,457 --> 00:03:08,205
Tip: a square meter is one hundred
centimeters by one hundred centimeters.

52
00:03:08,205 --> 00:03:11,799
That's ten thousand square centimeters,
not one hundred.

53
00:03:12,249 --> 00:03:17,539
Tip: convert lengths to meters before you
multiply. It avoids the squared-unit trap.

54
00:03:17,989 --> 00:03:22,973
Tip: always write the unit. A number
alone, like one point one, means nothing

55
00:03:22,973 --> 00:03:23,685
in physics.

56
00:03:24,835 --> 00:03:29,747
Your turn. A phone screen is fifteen
centimeters by seven centimeters. What is

57
00:03:29,747 --> 00:03:31,385
its area in square meters?

58
00:03:31,835 --> 00:03:33,029
Pause the video now.

59
00:03:33,479 --> 00:03:39,702
Fifteen centimeters is zero point one five
meters, and seven centimeters is zero

60
00:03:39,702 --> 00:03:45,692
point zero seven meters. Multiply to get
zero point zero one zero five square

61
00:03:45,692 --> 00:03:46,237
meters.

62
00:03:46,537 --> 00:03:50,974
The distance from Earth to the Sun is
about one hundred fifty billion meters.

63
00:03:51,424 --> 00:03:54,795
A human hair is about seventy millionths
of a meter wide.

64
00:03:55,245 --> 00:04:00,992
Writing all those zeros is slow, and easy
to get wrong. Scientific notation fixes

65
00:04:00,992 --> 00:04:01,346
that.

66
00:04:02,496 --> 00:04:07,946
In scientific notation, we write a number
as a value from one to ten, times a power

67
00:04:07,946 --> 00:04:08,406
of ten.

68
00:04:08,856 --> 00:04:14,020
One hundred fifty billion meters becomes
one point five times ten to the eleventh

69
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:14,466
meters.

70
00:04:14,916 --> 00:04:19,866
Seventy millionths of a meter becomes
seven times ten to the minus five meters.

71
00:04:21,016 --> 00:04:23,618
Every measurement also has limited
precision.

72
00:04:24,068 --> 00:04:28,874
With a ruler marked in centimeters, we
might read a pencil's length as fourteen

73
00:04:28,874 --> 00:04:30,212
point six centimeters.

74
00:04:30,662 --> 00:04:34,204
The one and the four are certain, and the
six is our best estimate.

75
00:04:34,654 --> 00:04:39,221
The digits we can trust are called
significant figures. This measurement has

76
00:04:39,221 --> 00:04:39,582
three.

77
00:04:40,732 --> 00:04:45,586
When you multiply or divide, round the
answer to the fewest significant figures

78
00:04:45,586 --> 00:04:46,385
in your data.

79
00:04:46,835 --> 00:04:52,528
For example, two point five meters times
three point four two meters is eight point

80
00:04:52,528 --> 00:04:54,174
five five square meters.

81
00:04:54,624 --> 00:04:59,279
But two point five has only two
significant figures, so we report eight

82
00:04:59,279 --> 00:05:00,853
point six square meters.

83
00:05:02,003 --> 00:05:07,443
To convert units, multiply by conversion
factors: fractions that are equal to one.

84
00:05:07,893 --> 00:05:12,074
Let's convert seventy two kilometers per
hour into meters per second.

85
00:05:12,524 --> 00:05:17,625
Multiply by one thousand meters over one
kilometer, and by one hour over three

86
00:05:17,625 --> 00:05:19,522
thousand six hundred seconds.

87
00:05:19,972 --> 00:05:24,068
The kilometers and the hours cancel,
leaving twenty meters per second.

88
00:05:25,218 --> 00:05:26,583
Now try one yourself.

89
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:30,596
Convert fifteen meters per second into
kilometers per hour.

90
00:05:31,046 --> 00:05:32,240
Pause the video now.

91
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:37,489
Multiply by three thousand six hundred
seconds per hour, and divide by one

92
00:05:37,489 --> 00:05:42,418
thousand meters per kilometer. The answer
is fifty four kilometers per hour.

93
00:05:42,718 --> 00:05:45,875
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

94
00:05:47,025 --> 00:05:52,081
Light travels at three point zero zero
times ten to the eight meters per second.

95
00:05:52,531 --> 00:05:57,267
The Sun is one point five zero times ten
to the eleven meters from Earth.

96
00:05:57,717 --> 00:06:01,451
How long does sunlight take to reach us,
in seconds and in minutes?

97
00:06:02,601 --> 00:06:04,713
Time is distance divided by speed.

98
00:06:05,163 --> 00:06:10,155
In scientific notation, divide the front
numbers, and subtract the powers of ten.

99
00:06:11,305 --> 00:06:17,677
Front numbers: one point five zero divided
by three point zero zero is zero point

100
00:06:17,677 --> 00:06:18,857
five zero zero.

101
00:06:19,307 --> 00:06:23,744
Powers: ten to the eleven divided by ten
to the eight is ten to the three.

102
00:06:24,194 --> 00:06:28,973
Together: zero point five times ten to the
three, which is five hundred seconds.

103
00:06:29,423 --> 00:06:34,437
Convert to minutes: five hundred divided
by sixty is about eight point three three

104
00:06:34,437 --> 00:06:34,927
minutes.

105
00:06:36,077 --> 00:06:40,581
Sunlight takes about five hundred seconds,
a little over eight minutes, to reach

106
00:06:40,581 --> 00:06:40,919
Earth.

107
00:06:41,369 --> 00:06:45,771
Sig figs: both inputs have three
significant figures, so the answer gets

108
00:06:45,771 --> 00:06:46,383
three too.

109
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:51,569
Tip: when dividing powers of ten, subtract
the exponents. Don't divide them.

110
00:06:52,019 --> 00:06:56,029
Tip: write the conversion factor as a
fraction, so the seconds cancel.

111
00:06:57,179 --> 00:07:02,046
Your turn. The Moon is three point eight
four times ten to the eight meters away.

112
00:07:02,046 --> 00:07:05,350
How long does light take to get from the
Moon to Earth?

113
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:06,995
Pause the video now.

114
00:07:07,445 --> 00:07:12,983
Divide: three point eight four over three
point zero zero is one point two eight,

115
00:07:12,983 --> 00:07:17,770
and the powers of ten cancel. So it takes
one point two eight seconds.

116
00:07:18,070 --> 00:07:20,865
You walk four meters east, then three
meters north.

117
00:07:21,315 --> 00:07:25,005
How far did you go? The answer depends on
what we mean by far.

118
00:07:25,455 --> 00:07:29,125
Physics has two different answers, called
distance and displacement.

119
00:07:30,275 --> 00:07:35,180
Some quantities only need a size. Mass,
time and temperature are like this. We

120
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:36,312
call them scalars.

121
00:07:36,762 --> 00:07:41,040
Other quantities need a size and a
direction. Pushes, velocities and

122
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:44,186
displacements are like this. We call them
vectors.

123
00:07:44,636 --> 00:07:48,998
We draw a vector as an arrow. Its length
shows the size, and it points in the

124
00:07:48,998 --> 00:07:49,564
direction.

125
00:07:50,714 --> 00:07:55,258
Distance is a scalar. It's the total
length of the path you actually traveled.

126
00:07:55,708 --> 00:07:59,783
In our walk, that's four meters plus three
meters, which is seven meters.

127
00:08:00,933 --> 00:08:05,494
Displacement is a vector. It's a straight
arrow from where you started to where you

128
00:08:05,494 --> 00:08:05,989
ended up.

129
00:08:06,439 --> 00:08:11,078
Here it forms the long side of a right
triangle, so we use the Pythagorean

130
00:08:11,078 --> 00:08:11,580
theorem.

131
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:16,870
Three squared plus four squared is twenty
five, and the square root of twenty five

132
00:08:16,870 --> 00:08:17,342
is five.

133
00:08:17,792 --> 00:08:21,141
So the displacement is five meters,
pointing north of east.

134
00:08:22,291 --> 00:08:25,833
Distance can never be smaller than the
size of the displacement.

135
00:08:26,283 --> 00:08:30,854
Run one full lap around a four hundred
meter track, and your distance is four

136
00:08:30,854 --> 00:08:31,744
hundred meters.

137
00:08:32,194 --> 00:08:35,543
But you end where you started, so your
displacement is zero.

138
00:08:36,693 --> 00:08:38,059
Now try one yourself.

139
00:08:38,509 --> 00:08:43,241
A dog runs ten meters east, then comes
back six meters west. What is its

140
00:08:43,241 --> 00:08:45,805
distance, and what is its displacement?

141
00:08:46,255 --> 00:08:47,449
Pause the video now.

142
00:08:47,899 --> 00:08:52,848
The distance is ten plus six, which is
sixteen meters. The displacement is ten

143
00:08:52,848 --> 00:08:55,195
minus six, which is four meters east.

144
00:08:55,496 --> 00:08:58,653
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

145
00:08:59,803 --> 00:09:03,899
A runner goes around a circular track with
a radius of fifty meters.

146
00:09:04,349 --> 00:09:08,744
Find the distance and displacement after
half a lap, and after one full lap.

147
00:09:09,894 --> 00:09:14,481
Distance is the length of the path. For a
circle, one lap is the circumference.

148
00:09:14,931 --> 00:09:19,091
Displacement is the straight line from
start to finish, with a direction.

149
00:09:20,241 --> 00:09:24,849
Full circumference: two pi times fifty,
about three hundred fourteen meters.

150
00:09:25,299 --> 00:09:29,843
Half a lap: the distance is half of that,
about one hundred fifty seven meters.

151
00:09:30,293 --> 00:09:35,288
After half a lap, the runner is straight
across the circle. The displacement is the

152
00:09:35,288 --> 00:09:37,034
diameter, one hundred meters.

153
00:09:37,484 --> 00:09:42,339
After a full lap: the distance is three
hundred fourteen meters, but the runner is

154
00:09:42,339 --> 00:09:45,121
back at the start, so the displacement is
zero.

155
00:09:46,271 --> 00:09:51,476
Half a lap: one hundred fifty seven meters
of distance, but only one hundred meters

156
00:09:51,476 --> 00:09:56,618
of displacement. A full lap: three hundred
fourteen meters, and zero displacement.

157
00:09:57,068 --> 00:10:01,906
Tip: displacement can never be bigger than
distance. At best they're equal, on a

158
00:10:01,906 --> 00:10:04,023
straight path with no turning back.

159
00:10:04,473 --> 00:10:07,609
Tip: displacement needs a direction.
Distance doesn't.

160
00:10:08,759 --> 00:10:13,960
Your turn. You walk around three sides of
a square city block, each side one hundred

161
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:16,993
meters long. Find your distance and
displacement.

162
00:10:17,443 --> 00:10:18,638
Pause the video now.

163
00:10:19,088 --> 00:10:24,131
The distance is three hundred meters. You
end up one corner away from the start, so

164
00:10:24,131 --> 00:10:27,899
the displacement is one hundred meters,
along the fourth side.

165
00:10:28,198 --> 00:10:31,313
A car's speedometer reads sixty kilometers
per hour.

166
00:10:31,763 --> 00:10:33,811
But is the car heading north, or south?

167
00:10:34,261 --> 00:10:36,885
Speed and velocity answer different
questions.

168
00:10:38,035 --> 00:10:42,557
Average speed is the total distance
traveled, divided by the time it took.

169
00:10:43,007 --> 00:10:45,887
A runner covers four hundred meters in
eighty seconds.

170
00:10:46,337 --> 00:10:51,548
The runner's average speed is four hundred
divided by eighty, which is five meters

171
00:10:51,548 --> 00:10:52,247
per second.

172
00:10:53,397 --> 00:10:57,109
Average velocity is the displacement,
divided by the time.

173
00:10:57,559 --> 00:11:02,622
Because displacement is a vector, velocity
is a vector too. It has a size, and a

174
00:11:02,622 --> 00:11:03,255
direction.

175
00:11:04,405 --> 00:11:08,479
Back to the runner. Those four hundred
meters were one full lap of the track.

176
00:11:08,929 --> 00:11:12,556
The runner ends where they started, so the
displacement is zero.

177
00:11:13,006 --> 00:11:18,224
That makes the average velocity zero, even
though the average speed was five meters

178
00:11:18,224 --> 00:11:18,915
per second.

179
00:11:20,065 --> 00:11:24,797
Instantaneous speed is how fast you're
going at a single moment. That's what a

180
00:11:24,797 --> 00:11:25,889
speedometer shows.

181
00:11:26,339 --> 00:11:29,603
Add a direction, and you have the
instantaneous velocity.

182
00:11:30,053 --> 00:11:34,299
If either the speed or the direction
changes, the velocity changes.

183
00:11:35,449 --> 00:11:36,814
Now try one yourself.

184
00:11:37,264 --> 00:11:42,553
A cyclist rides twelve hundred meters
north in four minutes. What is the average

185
00:11:42,553 --> 00:11:44,603
velocity, in meters per second?

186
00:11:45,053 --> 00:11:46,247
Pause the video now.

187
00:11:46,697 --> 00:11:52,019
Four minutes is two hundred forty seconds.
Twelve hundred divided by two hundred

188
00:11:52,019 --> 00:11:56,809
forty is five. So the average velocity is
five meters per second, north.

189
00:11:57,110 --> 00:12:00,267
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

190
00:12:01,417 --> 00:12:04,489
A car drives sixty kilometers north in one
hour.

191
00:12:04,939 --> 00:12:08,225
Then it drives thirty kilometers back
south, in half an hour.

192
00:12:08,675 --> 00:12:12,493
Find the average speed and the average
velocity for the whole trip.

193
00:12:13,643 --> 00:12:15,905
Average speed uses the total distance.

194
00:12:16,355 --> 00:12:18,787
Average velocity uses the displacement.

195
00:12:19,937 --> 00:12:23,841
Total time: one hour plus half an hour,
one point five hours.

196
00:12:24,291 --> 00:12:27,661
Total distance: sixty plus thirty, ninety
kilometers.

197
00:12:28,111 --> 00:12:32,485
Displacement: sixty north minus thirty
south, thirty kilometers north.

198
00:12:32,935 --> 00:12:38,033
Average speed: ninety divided by one point
five, sixty kilometers per hour.

199
00:12:38,483 --> 00:12:43,832
Average velocity: thirty divided by one
point five, twenty kilometers per hour

200
00:12:43,832 --> 00:12:44,243
north.

201
00:12:45,393 --> 00:12:50,558
The average speed is sixty kilometers per
hour, but the average velocity is only

202
00:12:50,558 --> 00:12:52,753
twenty kilometers per hour, north.

203
00:12:53,203 --> 00:12:58,211
Tip: don't average the two speeds. The
legs take different times, so sixty and

204
00:12:58,211 --> 00:12:59,945
sixty don't simply average.

205
00:13:00,395 --> 00:13:02,677
Tip: velocity always needs a direction.

206
00:13:03,827 --> 00:13:09,118
Your turn. A swimmer does one length of a
fifty meter pool and swims back, in eighty

207
00:13:09,118 --> 00:13:13,086
seconds total. Find the average speed and
the average velocity.

208
00:13:13,536 --> 00:13:14,731
Pause the video now.

209
00:13:15,181 --> 00:13:20,604
The distance is one hundred meters, so the
average speed is one hundred over eighty,

210
00:13:20,604 --> 00:13:25,833
one point two five meters per second. The
swimmer ends where they started, so the

211
00:13:25,833 --> 00:13:27,447
average velocity is zero.

212
00:13:27,897 --> 00:13:31,887
Next, in Part 2: acceleration, motion
graphs, and free fall.

213
00:13:33,037 --> 00:13:37,736
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
STEM, Explained, and tap the bell so you

214
00:13:37,736 --> 00:13:39,095
don't miss the next one.

